00:03:26.171 --> 00:03:28.327
- [Voiceover] SaltySnapz
asks, "People say the road

00:03:28.327 --> 00:03:29.795
"to success is lonely.

00:03:29.795 --> 00:03:31.187
"Do you feel that's accurate?

00:03:31.187 --> 00:03:33.874
"If not, why do you think
it's a common sentiment?"

00:03:34.748 --> 00:03:38.080
- You know, I think
people use that statement

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:40.061
because the truth is, I mean first of all,

00:03:40.061 --> 00:03:41.631
it depends on how you define success.

00:03:41.631 --> 00:03:43.317
In the context of what this show is about,

00:03:43.317 --> 00:03:44.594
which is business success,

00:03:44.594 --> 00:03:46.408
obviously we talk about a
lot of life stuff as well.

00:03:46.408 --> 00:03:49.026
But, you know, when you're the CEO,

00:03:49.026 --> 00:03:50.867
when you are the founder,

00:03:50.867 --> 00:03:52.538
the conversation that's not being had,

00:03:52.538 --> 00:03:54.697
everybody sees all the nice
things that come along.

00:03:54.697 --> 00:03:57.789
But every single thing that
happens in this company

00:03:57.789 --> 00:03:59.692
that's wrong is my fault.

00:03:59.692 --> 00:04:02.795
Every snarky comment on social.

00:04:02.795 --> 00:04:05.397
Every kind of, like, sad face.

00:04:05.397 --> 00:04:07.272
Every email bullet points that I,

00:04:07.272 --> 00:04:10.177
every Friday I get bullet
points from tons of

00:04:10.177 --> 00:04:12.846
Vayner Media employees and 80% of them

00:04:12.846 --> 00:04:15.398
have a bullet that's like,
"Rick is stretched too thin

00:04:15.398 --> 00:04:16.637
"and is crying in the bathroom."

00:04:16.637 --> 00:04:17.954
That's devastating.

00:04:17.954 --> 00:04:20.685
It's very tough to be
at the top of something,

00:04:20.685 --> 00:04:22.084
even when it's going great.

00:04:22.084 --> 00:04:23.705
Vayner's going great.

00:04:23.705 --> 00:04:25.824
This is not even, like, you
know Wall Street collapsed

00:04:25.824 --> 00:04:28.268
and we lost a lot of clients
and we have to have layoffs.

00:04:28.268 --> 00:04:30.216
This is, like, we're
rolling and we're the best

00:04:30.216 --> 00:04:33.497
and we're crushing and there's
always things to worry about.

00:04:33.497 --> 00:04:35.853
Because you know that there's no,

00:04:35.853 --> 00:04:37.715
"Oh, it's India's fault."

00:04:37.715 --> 00:04:41.496
Everybody here, at some
level, all the way up

00:04:41.496 --> 00:04:44.151
can still say (snaps
fingers), "It's Gary's fault."

00:04:44.151 --> 00:04:46.947
I can't and when you say it's my fault,

00:04:46.947 --> 00:04:49.605
there's a really tough burden emotionally,

00:04:49.605 --> 00:04:51.992
forget about financially,
that comes along with that.

00:04:51.992 --> 00:04:54.585
And so I think it can get
very lonely, you do recognize.

00:04:54.585 --> 00:04:57.063
It's amazing to me, for
as much of a communinary

00:04:57.063 --> 00:05:00.281
and extrovert as I am, how
much I keep in my own mind.

00:05:00.281 --> 00:05:02.976
How much is going on in
this noggin every day.

00:05:02.976 --> 00:05:05.274
Calculating, strategizing, thinking.

00:05:05.274 --> 00:05:06.795
And there is no vacations.

00:05:06.795 --> 00:05:10.510
I desperately love national holidays

00:05:10.510 --> 00:05:14.571
and, like, I can't wait for
Christmas and Thanksgiving

00:05:14.571 --> 00:05:16.422
because everybody else is checked out

00:05:16.422 --> 00:05:19.756
and that's the only time,
that's the only time

00:05:19.756 --> 00:05:21.072
that I'm able to be off.

00:05:21.072 --> 00:05:23.077
I have my 40th birthday
coming on Saturday.

00:05:23.077 --> 00:05:25.122
I'm going away with my family.

00:05:25.122 --> 00:05:27.279
This is an incredible moment in my life

00:05:27.279 --> 00:05:30.025
and I will not be able
to be 100% checked out.

00:05:30.025 --> 00:05:32.771
Because then those, you know
and that Friday and that Monday

00:05:32.771 --> 00:05:34.775
the world is moving, this world is moving.

00:05:34.775 --> 00:05:36.576
My responsibilities are moving.

00:05:36.576 --> 00:05:38.759
I'm always one phone call away from a fire

00:05:38.759 --> 00:05:40.164
that I have to address.

00:05:40.164 --> 00:05:43.952
It is a very intense, lonely
place to be, at the tipy top.

00:05:43.952 --> 00:05:46.060
And so, when you think about,

00:05:46.060 --> 00:05:48.048
you know, I always think
about, like, "Is life fair?"

00:05:48.048 --> 00:05:49.196
Right?

00:05:49.196 --> 00:05:50.690
There's always things that happen,

00:05:50.690 --> 00:05:52.210
there's things that we can't control.

00:05:52.210 --> 00:05:55.354
But the notion of, like,
somebody getting compensated

00:05:55.354 --> 00:05:57.276
a lot of money for things that we,

00:05:57.276 --> 00:05:59.122
do I think somebody making
20 million dollars a year

00:05:59.122 --> 00:06:01.102
to be an athlete or an actress.

00:06:01.102 --> 00:06:03.147
We never talk about
actresses and actors, right?

00:06:03.147 --> 00:06:05.700
We love to zing on athletes
but, like, actors make, like,

00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:08.088
the big ones make like
eight, 15 million dollars

00:06:08.088 --> 00:06:09.301
to make a movie.

00:06:09.301 --> 00:06:10.731
But still, at the end of the day,

00:06:10.731 --> 00:06:12.774
it's because they command the market.

00:06:12.774 --> 00:06:13.886
People want to pay attention

00:06:13.886 --> 00:06:15.546
and there's enormous amount of pressure.

00:06:15.546 --> 00:06:17.334
As I've lived my life and
started spending some time with

00:06:17.334 --> 00:06:21.113
A-list celebrities, there
life is, I mean, it's intense.

00:06:21.113 --> 00:06:23.054
Like, I really secretly
think that I could be

00:06:23.054 --> 00:06:24.969
an A-list celebrity.

00:06:24.969 --> 00:06:27.345
That I literally could go
on TV and be a breakout hit.

00:06:27.345 --> 00:06:30.346
Like in a Mad Money
kind of like, you know,

00:06:31.236 --> 00:06:32.817
not like Jennifer Lawrence.

00:06:32.817 --> 00:06:34.158
I just don't have the looks.

00:06:34.158 --> 00:06:37.416
But in a Mad Money, kind of like Regis,

00:06:37.416 --> 00:06:40.928
kind of like Andy Cohen way.

00:06:40.928 --> 00:06:44.835
But man, real celebrity status is intense.

00:06:44.835 --> 00:06:47.186
You just have nowhere to
go and so there's that.

00:06:47.186 --> 00:06:50.185
Or an athlete where the
physical shape that you have to,

00:06:50.185 --> 00:06:53.248
18, you know, 10 hours a
day of putting in that time.

00:06:53.248 --> 00:06:55.098
People look at the outcomes
they don't think about

00:06:55.098 --> 00:06:57.508
what's coming along with
it and the pressure.

00:06:57.508 --> 00:06:59.706
The pressure to know
that if I get hit wrong,

00:06:59.706 --> 00:07:00.957
that my entire life collapses,

00:07:00.957 --> 00:07:03.602
especially an athlete
that has a short window.

00:07:03.602 --> 00:07:05.437
Or the pressures that we've, look,

00:07:05.437 --> 00:07:08.046
I don't think it's a coincidence
that some of the greatest

00:07:08.046 --> 00:07:10.761
artists of all time pass
away in their mid-20s

00:07:10.761 --> 00:07:12.497
because of the intensity.

00:07:12.497 --> 00:07:14.478
It's intense, it's intense.

00:07:14.478 --> 00:07:17.517
It's very lonely and so it
could be extremely lonely.

00:07:17.517 --> 00:07:20.556
I, ironically, and you'll
find this wild, I love,

00:07:20.556 --> 00:07:23.750
look I want random extra
people in the room.

00:07:23.750 --> 00:07:26.297
So, like, I love being around
people more than anything

00:07:26.297 --> 00:07:28.235
but I'm also very comfortable in my own,

00:07:28.235 --> 00:07:30.315
being one-on-one with myself.

00:07:30.315 --> 00:07:33.508
So I'm good but I know that I'm

00:07:33.508 --> 00:07:37.466
emotionally stable as fuck
and it's intense for me.

00:07:37.466 --> 00:07:41.321
I can't imagine people that
are not as fortunate as I am

00:07:41.321 --> 00:07:42.381
pulling from both directions.

00:07:42.381 --> 00:07:44.322
Ambition but humil, you know.

00:07:44.322 --> 00:07:47.379
Like, do you know how content I am?

00:07:47.379 --> 00:07:49.469
A lot of people watch
this show and you hear

00:07:49.469 --> 00:07:52.906
I wanna buy the Jets and you misunderstand

00:07:52.906 --> 00:07:54.822
really where I'm at.

00:07:54.822 --> 00:07:57.477
I want it all and I
wanna win the whole thing

00:07:57.477 --> 00:08:01.040
but if I never win again,
if I plateau at this level,

00:08:01.040 --> 00:08:03.786
there's an amazing amount
of content in my body

00:08:03.786 --> 00:08:05.241
for all my hunger.

00:08:07.091 --> 00:08:09.346
And that balances me
but I couldn't imagine

00:08:09.346 --> 00:08:11.108
if that was tweaked just a little bit.

00:08:11.108 --> 00:08:13.394
If I really felt the pressure

00:08:13.394 --> 00:08:14.646
because there's enough pressure from

00:08:14.646 --> 00:08:15.843
a day in and day out standpoint.

00:08:15.843 --> 00:08:17.683
It's intense, it's intense.

00:08:17.683 --> 00:08:20.262
I think people say it I
think much like stereotypes

00:08:20.262 --> 00:08:22.427
or statements there's
always so many truth to it.

00:08:22.427 --> 00:08:23.716
I think the reason so many people say

00:08:23.716 --> 00:08:27.319
the road is lonely because
to really be successful,

00:08:27.319 --> 00:08:29.128
in the context of business especially,

00:08:29.128 --> 00:08:32.539
you've gotta make seven to 7,000 decisions

00:08:32.539 --> 00:08:36.504
that are critical and you can
only make them with yourself.

00:08:37.403 --> 00:08:40.104
It's an intense moment on a daily basis.

00:08:42.115 --> 00:08:43.102
Yeah.

